diff --git a/docs/remedy-storage-and-reads.md b/docs/remedy-storage-and-reads.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..64abfa16
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/remedy-storage-and-reads.md
@@ -0,0 +1,149 @@
+# Bounded Remedy reads and immutable operation events (LT-157)
+
+This is a development delivery candidate. Production writes remain on hold.
+Default-disabled Remedy still creates no optional models/collections at import.
+No startup migration, history deletion, retained-data rewrite or replica-set
+transaction is introduced.
+
+## Read contract
+
+All routes use existing verified-session, current project/organization policy and
+feature opt-in. Limits default to100 and reject values outside1–200. Cursors are
+opaque continuations bound to the project and selected identity. Object IDs
+provide deterministic history order; conformance selection separately retains
+createdAt descending then _id descending, regardless of review `at`.
+
+| GET suffix under /project/:projectID/accessibility/workqueue | Result |
+|---|---|
+| /conformance | Global verification counts/digests and up to100 page summaries; `nextCursor` continues pages. No source scans, reviewer notes or repeated checklists. |
+| /conformance/pages/:sectionID | One selected page's scan evidence and compact criterion statuses. |
+| /conformance/pages/:sectionID/criteria/:criterionID | One selected criterion, checklist and current reviewer evidence. |
+| /conformance/history?sectionID=…&criterionID=… | Up to100 immutable review records with `nextCursor`. Concurrent later appends cannot shift the captured ID range. |
+| (base) | Up to100 work-item summaries without history/evidence arrays; `nextCursor`, `nextSectionCursor`, `nextCandidateCursor` expose continuation rather than truncation. `sectionCursor`/`candidateCursor` continue those lists. |
+| /:itemID/history?kind=history or scanEvidence | Up to100 retained array events, newest first, with stable `historyID` and a captured append-only array boundary. |
+| /page/:sectionID/operations/:localID/history | Original actor with current edit permission, or support/superadmin project policy; up to100 immutable events, with preserved legacy events and explicit continuation. |
+
+The mounted UI requests selected detail/history only when needed, and supplies
+explicit continuation controls. Scan-all explicitly walks every page-summary
+batch. It never treats the first100 as a complete book. Work-item note/edit and
+human verification semantics remain unchanged.
+
+Selection reads stream only minimal review identity/status metadata, once, using
+the project/kind/history-order index. A hash lookup per record preserves the first
+matching scope/fingerprint for each page/criterion; duplicate stored section IDs
+retain their distinct fingerprint matches. Superseded notes are not loaded.
+Only selected reviewer details are fetched by ID. Application state is bounded by
+the number of stored pages×50 criteria, rather than all historical reviews. Mongo
+reads and the metadata selection loop have a30second budget; unavailable/over-budget
+reads fail explicitly. Metadata exceeding2000 title characters or8192 URL
+characters is refused in summaries rather than silently changed; full export
+preserves the original values.
+
+## Complete exports
+
+| GET suffix | Access and contents |
+|---|---|
+| /conformance/report | Existing authorized project readers; full page evaluation plus complete immutable `recordHistory`. |
+| /items/report | Existing authorized project readers; all work items and complete retained note/scan histories. |
+| /operations/report | Support/superadmin with existing project policy only; all immutable operation events and verbatim legacy Project history. Ordinary conformance exports never disclose private operation bindings/session associations. |
+
+Exports stream JSON with HTTP backpressure, bounded Mongo batches (100 records,
+20 work items), an explicit120second total deadline and client cancellation.
+Only a completed download contains `complete:true`. Failure/cancellation closes
+the connection and cursor; there is no success footer or silent truncation.
+Conformance exports capture a record-ID boundary and evaluation time, then emit
+full page details one at a time. Records appended after that boundary are left
+for a later export. Work-item fields are per-document reads, not a cross-document
+transaction snapshot; drain writers for a backup-grade consistent inventory.
+
+Cursor disposal is deliberately idempotent. Concurrent explicit closes can end
+an implicit Mongo driver session before its first killCursors executes. An actual
+HTTP cancellation test checks Mongo's server cursor inventory, not merely a
+cancelled browser response. Stream cursors use this single explicit lifecycle;
+the same driver abort signal is not attached to their close path. Short queries
+use their abort signal and Mongo maxTimeMS.
+
+## Write-ahead operation protocol; standalone Mongo supported
+
+1. Persist an immutable intent in `remedyoperationrecords`, using journaled
+   majority write acknowledgement with a10second acknowledgement timeout.
+2. CAS the Project's exact reviewed preview, membership policy, canonical target,
+   evidence version and pending pointer. Only a successful acknowledged CAS may
+   dispatch the signed operation. The Project holds one active local ID per
+   section; new history is never appended to Project.remedyOperations.
+3. Append the immutable response before the local result CAS. A failed append or
+   ambiguous acknowledgement leaves the pending claim held. A later status read
+   never clears it or repeats a mutation.
+
+Both intent/response appends and the Project CAS use explicit journaled majority
+acknowledgement. These operations work on Mongo7.0.14 standalone; no session
+transaction or replica-set prerequisite is hidden. Replica topologies must still
+satisfy their own majority/availability policy. A failed CAS can leave an orphan
+intent, which is preserved and never dispatched. An ambiguous CAS can leave a
+held pointer with no remote operation; missing remote status is not proof that a
+claim may be unlocked. LT-127's separately authorized resolution remains required.
+Original actor, action, binding, issuer/jti and outcomes are never rewritten.
+
+New event metadata is capped at64KiB before append. Conformance record writes
+have the same explicit64KiB budget. Work items retain their existing schema with
+explicit refusal: fewer than1000 events in each history/evidence array and less
+than8MiB before an append; each new event is at most64KiB. The same Mongo CAS
+checks those limits, so concurrent appends cannot race beyond the budget. This
+leaves substantial room below Mongo's16MiB document ceiling. A full item stays
+readable/exportable; appends require an operator's separately reviewed retention
+or storage expansion decision. No automatic compaction, truncation or deletion
+occurs, and a completed page write must never be repeated because task evidence
+could not fit.
+
+## Collections, indexes and privileges
+
+| Collection | Declared indexes in addition to _id |
+|---|---|
+| conformancerecords | `{projectID:1,kind:1,createdAt:-1,_id:-1}`; `{projectID:1,sectionID:1,criterionID:1,_id:-1}` |
+| remedyoperationrecords | `{projectID:1,localID:1,kind:1,_id:-1}`; unique `{projectID:1,localID:1}` for kind=intent; unique `{issuer:1,jti:1}` for kind=intent |
+| remediationworkitems | Existing unique `{projectID:1,key:1}` |
+
+Old declared indexes are preserved; no index is dropped. The enabled feature's
+health/queue preflight explicitly creates collections and declared indexes. Matrix
+writes also ensure operation indexes before intent admission. Models disable
+autoCreate/autoIndex. Initialization failure reports unavailable; disabled Remedy
+does not initialize them.
+
+The configured account currently needs collection/index creation privileges for
+enabled preflight, read/insert on append-only record collections, and normal
+Project/work-item read/update privileges. The app never updates/deletes operation
+or conformance records. Do not claim insert-only credentials suffice for current
+preflight, or that a production Atlas role/topology has been validated. Prepare
+collection-scoped roles/index windows with the deployment owner before opt-in.
+
+## Legacy compatibility, backup and rollback
+
+Legacy Project.remedyOperations remains select:false, immutable and readable by
+the authorized operation mapping/history/export paths. No backfill is necessary
+for compatibility. The read-only inventory command is:
+
+```sh
+# Use an operator-provided read-only URI in this variable; never print it.
+CONDUCTOR_REMEDY_DRY_RUN_URI="$operator_readonly_uri" \
+  node --import tsx scripts/inspectLegacyRemedyOperations.ts
+```
+
+It emits project IDs, legacy event/byte counts, a BSON SHA-256 inventory hash and
+pending-section counts. It makes no update, insert, collection/index or delete
+call. There is no `--apply` mode. A future backfill requires its own reviewed plan
+with exact identity/deduplication, dry-run evidence and owner authorization.
+
+Drain admissions and settle/retain pending claims before a consistent backup.
+Back up Projects, remediationworkitems, conformancerecords and
+remedyoperationrecords with referenced user identities and coordinate the same
+checkpoint with Bridge's independent ledger/fence, snapshots and trust epoch.
+Never restore one history collection or clear a pointer in isolation. A restore
+must preserve consumed nonce/token tombstones and all uncertain outcomes.
+
+Runtime rollback must retain access to the new collection and current pending
+mapping. The previous twenty-one-patch runtime cannot resolve new externalized
+intents. Keep this runtime available for read-only recovery, disable reviewed
+writes and preserve all stores; a downgrade compatibility adapter/operational
+resolution is a separate reviewed action. Never copy events back into Project,
+delete history or unlock/retry merely to make an older runtime appear healthy.
+No production migration, deployment or credential provisioning is authorized here.
